		{
			"file":"9. Misc/attachments/Quantum-mechanical effects in photoluminescence from thin crystalline gold films 1.pdf",
			"id":"7e9e5a56b206d135",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"type":"file",
			"x":1600,
			"y":1340,
			"width":400,
			"height":400
		},
		{
			"id":"dffd091a014b1956",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"### Revisiting the Integral\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(\\hbar\\omega) = \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(E + \\hbar\\omega)\\left[1 - f(E)\\right]\\,dE,\n$$\nwhere the Fermi-Dirac distribution is:\n$$\nf(E) = \\frac{1}{e^{\\beta(E - \\mu)} + 1}, \\quad \\beta = \\frac{1}{k_{\\text{B}} T}.\n$$\n\n---\n### Step 2: Product of the Terms\nThe product becomes:\n$$\nf(E + \\hbar\\omega)\\left[1 - f(E)\\right] = \\frac{e^{\\beta(E - \\mu)}}{\\left(e^{\\beta(E - \\mu)} + 1\\right)\\left(e^{\\beta(E + \\hbar\\omega - \\mu)} + 1\\right)}.\n$$\n---\n### Step 3: Substitution of Variables\nLet us substitute:\n$$\nx = \\beta(E - \\mu), \\quad E = \\mu + \\frac{x}{\\beta}, \\quad dE = \\frac{dx}{\\beta},\n$$\n\nand define:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\beta\\hbar\\omega.\n$$\n\nThe integral becomes:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(\\hbar\\omega) = \\frac{1}{\\beta} \\int_{x = -\\beta\\mu}^{\\infty} \\frac{e^{x}}{\\left(e^{x} + 1\\right)\\left(e^{x + \\Delta} + 1\\right)}\\,dx.\n$$\n\n---\n### Step 4: Simplification\nRecognizing the dependence on ( $\\mu$ ), let us analyze two cases:\n\n1. **General case ( $\\mu$ ) arbitrary):** The integral depends explicitly on $( \\mu )$, as seen from the lower limit $x = -\\beta\\mu$.\n2. **Degenerate limit $\\mu \\gg k_{\\text{B}} T$ :** When $\\mu \\gg k_{\\text{B}} T$, the term $e^{-\\beta\\mu} \\to 0$, and the lower limit $x = -\\beta\\mu \\to -\\infty$. In this regime, the dependence on $\\mu$ effectively drops out.\n\nUnder the degenerate limit, the integral simplifies to:\n$$\n\\Gamma(\\hbar\\omega) \\approx \\frac{1}{\\beta} \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{e^{x}}{\\left(e^{x} + 1\\right)\\left(e^{x + \\Delta} + 1\\right)}\\,dx.\n$$\n---\n### Step 5: Evaluation of the Integral\nUsing the identity:\n$$\n\\frac{e^{x}}{\\left(e^{x} + 1\\right)\\left(e^{x + \\Delta} + 1\\right)} = \\frac{1}{e^{\\Delta} - 1} \\left( \\frac{1}{e^{x} + 1} - \\frac{1}{e^{x + \\Delta} + 1} \\right),\n$$\nthe integral becomes:\n$$\n\\Gamma(\\hbar\\omega) \\approx \\frac{1}{\\beta} \\cdot \\frac{1}{e^{\\Delta} - 1} \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\left[ \\frac{1}{e^{x} + 1} - \\frac{1}{e^{x + \\Delta} + 1} \\right]\\,dx.\n$$\n---\n### Step 6: Simplification\n\nThe integrals can be evaluated, and it is found that:\n\n$$\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\left[ \\frac{1}{e^{x} + 1} - \\frac{1}{e^{x + \\Delta} + 1} \\right]\\,dx = \\Delta.\n$$\nThus, the integral becomes:\n\n$$\\Gamma(\\hbar\\omega) = \\frac{\\Delta}{\\beta} \\cdot \\frac{1}{e^{\\Delta} - 1}.\n$$\nSubstituting back \\Delta = \\beta\\hbar\\omega  we find:\n\n$$\\Gamma(\\hbar\\omega) = \\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{e^{\\beta\\hbar\\omega} - 1}.\n$$\n---\n\n### Final Result\nThe analytic result for the integral in the degenerate limit $\\mu \\gg k_{\\text{B}} T$ is:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(\\hbar\\omega) = \\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{e^{\\hbar\\omega / k_{\\text{B}} T} - 1}.\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Discussion on \\( \\mu \\)-Dependence\n\n- **Degenerate Limit (\\( \\mu \\gg k_{\\text{B}} T \\)):** The dependence on \\( \\mu \\) effectively disappears, and the integral simplifies to the above result.\n\n- **General Case:** For systems where \\( \\mu \\) is not much larger than \\( k_{\\text{B}} T \\), the integral depends explicitly on \\( \\mu \\), and a numerical evaluation would generally be required.\n\n",
			"type":"text",
			"x":2480,
			"y":160,
			"width":1080,
			"height":2240
